{
  "abstract": "A six-relation component in the rational Diophantine septuple problem leads to a genus-six entry curve with a three-dimensional Prym and a genus-fourteen cover retaining the individual square roots. We prove that the full-root cover has exactly eighteen rational normalization points, all above nine known boundary points. First we remove the class-group hypothesis from an earlier rank-two Prym theorem. A compactly supported test function in the explicit formula for a nontrivial unramified quadratic Hecke character has no zeta pole term and nonnegative zero contributions without GRH. Explicit principal generators for 21,846 prime ideals, combined with pessimistic character values at all other supported ideals, give a rigorously positive margin and prove the required S-class two-torsion vanishes. The generators are independently verified by exact norms and ideal membership. Next, the two unresolved eleven-adic Chabauty candidates determine characters in the order-eleven subgroup of the Prym reduction at thirty-one. The finite character table forces them into nodal boundary disks, where certified annihilating integrals have exact zero constant and unit linear coefficient. Each disk contains only its central zero. Exact normalization then supplies the eighteen rational lifts, including points at poles of the square-root character. A portable supplement reconstructs the prime coverage, interval bounds and local arithmetic. The result excludes ordinary septuples on the guarded native component represented by the decoder; its separate exceptional-place audit is retained. Other components and the general rational septuple problem remain open.",
